    "case_id": "591",
    "case_summary": "A 39-year-old Hispanic man presented with chest pain and shortness of breath for 12 to 24 hours. Past medical history significant for acute myocardial infarction 9 years ago at age 30. Vital signs were stable with blood pressure 127/86 mm Hg, heart rate 56 beats/min, respiratory rate 17 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation 97% on room air.\n\nPhysical examination findings were unremarkable, without signs of acute heart failure.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * High-sensitivity troponin T: 888 ng/L (normal range, 0–14 ng/L)    * Hypercoagulability work-up: No abnormalities  - Imaging Studies:    * Electrocardiogram: No apparent abnormalities on current admission    * Echocardiogram: Normal biventricular size and systolic function, no regional wall motion abnormalities, no pericardial effusion    * Coronary Angiogram: Widely patent RCA; left anterior descending artery showed a large filling defect in the proximal segment with TIMI flow grade 3    * Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT): No evidence of superficial lipid, showed findings in proximal segment  - Image Descriptions:    * Figure 1: Electrocardiogram from 9 years ago showing ST-segment elevation in II, III, aVF, V5, and V6 leads    * Figure 2: Coronary angiogram and OCT images from 9 years ago    * Figure 3: Current angiogram and OCT images    * Video 1: OCT at current presentation (Duration: 0:51)",
